My calls:
- Its own module, with no Apply.pm forwarders: t/52 holds Apply.pm under 700 lines, and Workloads stays NVIDIA-only.
- Removal: a component no marker node selects is removed if OCP recorded it OR its DaemonSet is in the cluster. That covers the last AMD node gone, gpu.enabled false, labeller/metrics off, and a switched allocation. This is asymmetric with the NVIDIA operator, which nothing removes. Reasons: a plugin left on a node switched to dra would hand out the GPU twice, and these are only a few kube-system objects. A Node list that fails removes nothing.
- Order: removals first (DRA before the plugin), then the narrow In component, then the default's NotIn component, then labeller and metrics. A switch never puts both allocation components on one node.
- Hash = md5 of canonical JSON {call, options (version, affinity, resource_naming, wait, node_labeller), Rex::Rancher::K8s $VERSION}. A Rex::Rancher upgrade therefore re-applies each component once. Unchanged = same hash AND DaemonSet present. The hash is saved only after Ready.
- Ready check (300s, 10s interval): DaemonSet observedGeneration/desired>0/numberReady/updatedNumberScheduled. Plugin: status.allocatable amd.com/gpu (mixed: any amd.com/*) > 0 on every node its affinity selects. DRA: a ResourceSlice of driver gpu.amd.com per such node. Labeller and metrics: the DaemonSet only; /metrics is not probed. Capacity is never read.
- The can guard checks every function this run needs before any call. If one is missing: one [WARN] on STDERR, nothing deployed or removed, not a failure.
- DRA without resource.k8s.io/v1: [!!] on STDERR with the lib's message plus a hint (no GPU on dra nodes until 1.34; use device-plugin meanwhile). Counts as a failed component; the others carry on.
- An allocation label that is none of the three values: [WARN] per node; the node gets the default (what NotIn does anyway).
- after_join: runs again only if the first step ran clean (no failure, no warn) and a joined Ready node carries the marker. No polling: the marker comes at registration.
- node add --gpu: polls for the AMD marker or the NFD NVIDIA label (300s). It ensures the AMD stack and/or the GPU Operator, whatever shows at the first poll that shows anything. A mixed host whose marker shows first gets the GPU Operator only with the next ocp apply. If neither shows: STDERR names both labels and says what each means.
- Metrics: wired per rex-rancher 4f2f3ca (marker affinity only, gated by gpu.amd.metrics, else removed). The "unwired" marker is gone.
- amd.com/gpu, amd.com/ and gpu.amd.com are local variables in AMDGPU.pm with a k258 comment. OCP::Config is ocp-state-worker's lane.

Lib gaps (rex-rancher board):
- deploy_amd_dra_driver and deploy_amd_metrics_exporter use the init image busybox:1.36. It is unpinned in OCP::Versions and has no override option, which contradicts the spec's "nothing unpinned".
- _wait_for_gpu_resource (deploy_amd_device_plugin wait=>1) polls status.capacity, not allocatable. OCP passes wait=>0, so OCP is unaffected.
- The image ships Rex::Rancher 0.004 (cpanfile requires 0.004). No released Rex::Rancher has the AMD functions. Until a release plus a cpanfile/snapshot bump, an AMD cluster gets the [WARN] and nothing is deployed.

